#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119420 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"}] :question/id #uuid "cbabcd64-6a33-552d-9f2f-704a9df44d1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10906" :question/problem "The set $\\{\\mathbf{a_1},\\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be $\\mathbf{a}_2$ if\n$\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_2 = 0\n$$\n\nOtherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nTo see why, notice that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nIn other words, the vectors $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are linearly dependent, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3}a_{1} - a_{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 +x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac 1 3\n$$\n\nand $x_2=-1$, which is different from $x_1=x_2=0$. Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119421 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/id #uuid "7b28b42f-0ac4-543e-baeb-51534aaad480" :question/math-academy-id "e-10907" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{a}_1 - \\mathbf{a}_2 -2 \\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$.\n2. $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$ only if $x_1=x_2=x_3 = 0$.\n3. The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{v}_3= \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0\n$$\n\nOtherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n$a_{1} - a_{2} - 2a_{3}|= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} - 2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}4 + 0 - 4 \\\\ -1 - 1 + 2 \\\\ 0 + 0 - 14\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}; \\ne 0$\n- Statement II is true. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n${4x_{1} + 2x_{3} = 0; - x_{1} + x_{2} - x_{3} = 0; 7x_{3} = 0$.\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n${-x_{1} + x_{2} - x_{3} = 0; 4x_{1} + 2x_{3} = 0; 7x_{3} = 0$\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0$.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119422 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/id #uuid "3843dc13-0908-5d4c-adfe-836a928a1410" :question/math-academy-id "e-10908" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nAssume that $x_1,x_2$, and $x_3$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n2. The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2$, and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent.\n3. The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2$, and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2$, and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3 \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0\n$$\n\nOtherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{2x_{1} - x_{2} + 2x_{3} = 0; 4x_{1} + 4x_{2} + 5x_{3} = 0; 6x_{2} + x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-1)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are $x_1$ and $x_2$, while $x_3$ is the free variable.\n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, statements II is false, while statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82e4016c-e9e0-57b5-8062-ccca5dd23da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117010} {:answer/id #uuid "95793e59-48c2-52e7-948b-d75ffe44549b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117011} {:answer/id #uuid "d86af066-4660-5ed8-bfdd-ece63aebb0da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117012} {:answer/id #uuid "41c0bea6-787a-5ece-84ee-d86aa4c21c76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117013} {:answer/id #uuid "4eda4f57-4838-58b3-9271-3c47e10adc90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41c0bea6-787a-5ece-84ee-d86aa4c21c76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117013} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a65dbe9-5584-55fe-8006-984cd0184c94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117009}] :question/id #uuid "d522f4eb-6fdb-5796-8d35-98401ee7b35b" :question/math-academy-id "q-156591" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{1} + a_{2} - 2a_{3} = 0$.\n2. $x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} + x_{3}a_{3} = 0$ only if $x_{1} = x_{2} = x_{3} = 0$.\n3. The set ${a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$ is linearly independent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0af6cce-cd06-5758-a89a-6a51369fcda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118025} {:answer/id #uuid "edb859c3-c8f8-529f-bd52-16a41793ca39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118026} {:answer/id #uuid "064fe1c8-24f4-5298-9309-5558161860fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118027} {:answer/id #uuid "20eda10f-a2d1-5cd2-8769-f3d86730499a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118028} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd631a4-527d-52a4-a2ad-53e14023cd9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cd631a4-527d-52a4-a2ad-53e14023cd9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118029} :answer-field/id #uuid "d94319c3-5829-5f1a-8376-0a32686004e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118024}] :question/id #uuid "24ebfa1a-3835-5c3f-9168-f1537d003724" :question/math-academy-id "q-34291" :question/problem "The set ${a_{1}, a_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be $a_{2}$ if\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$?"}]}
